      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A lexical item ni'nvit, nenve'thicnin is glossed 'evil spirit'.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "The noun ne'La is glossed 'herd'.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Verbal forms describe hunting and fishing actions: ilva'-iiiti'ykm means 'to hunt wild reindeer' and qata'p-niti'ykm means 'to catch winter fish'.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Several lexical items describe smoke and smoky conditions, including 'smoke', 'smoky', 'to be smoky, to feel smoky', 'to make a smouldering fire with plenty of smoke', and 'to be full of smoke'.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The noun li'li is glossed 'egg' and given with a Chukchi cognate.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The verb nalqi'wekin means 'to sit down upon a sledge (mostly astride)'.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Personal pronouns and related forms are listed: gum, gu'mma ('I' intransitive subject), gum-na'n ('I' transitive subject), gumnan cini't ('myself'), and gum-ni'n ('my, mine').",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Quantitative and distributive expressions appear, such as ga'mga- meaning 'every, each' and ga'mga-qlawuj meaning 'every man'.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Emotional or psychological verbs are present: gaima'tekm ('to desire') and gaimiyo'oykm ('to be joyful').",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Marriage-related expressions include fia'wan ('wife'), naw-i-nyu'ykm ('to woo, to ask for a wife', literally 'thou herdest [the reindeer-herd], for a wife'), and ya-nawt-i-fia't-i-kin ('thou makest him to have the wife').",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Verbs describe lack and suffering (nova'ykm, 'to lack something, to be suffering') and movement (fiito'-ykin, 'to go out').",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Numerals and related forms for 'two' and 'three' are included, with plural constructions for 'they two' and 'they three', a numeral verb, and an interjection 'the deuce!' combined with verbs.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:15"
      ]
    }
